<section xml:id="applying-binomial-expansion-to-approximate-exponential-numbers">
  <title>
    Applying Binomial Expansion to Approximate Exponential Numbers
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Did you know we can use binomial expansion to approximate exponential
      numbers? This powerful technique allows us to simplify complex
      expressions and make calculations easier. We will explore how to apply
      binomial expansion to approximate exponential functions.
    </p>

    <activity>
      <p>
        Activity 1.6: Approximating Exponential Numbers Using Binomial
        Expansion <m>(a+b)^n</m> Ensure you have your notebook, calculator and
        pen ready for the activity.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Choose a number to work with. Examples include: 1.5, 3.5, 10,
              etc.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Express your chosen number in the form <m>(a+b)</m>. For
              example: if you chose 1.5, you could write it as <m>(1+0.5)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Select any integer to represent <m>n</m>. Examples include: 2,
              4, <m>-4</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Write your decimal and <m>n</m> in the form <m>(a+b)^n</m>. For
              example, using 1.5 and <m>n=4</m>, you would write
              <m>(1+0.5)^4</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Apply the binomial theorem to expand and evaluate your
              expression.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Choose different sets of numbers and repeat the steps above.
              Experiment with various decimal values and <m>n</m> to see how
              the approximations change.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use your calculator to find the actual value of your chosen
              decimal raised to the power of <m>n</m>. For example, calculate
              <m>1.5^4</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Look at the results from your binomial expansion and the
              calculator.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Discuss:</p>

            <p>
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    How close were your approximations to the actual values?
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    What did you observe about the accuracy of your
                    approximations with different <m>n</m> values?
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </activity>

    <p>
      Great work! You have effectively used binomial expansion to approximate
      exponential numbers. Now, let us solve more examples together to deepen
      your understanding!
    </p>

    <example>
      <p>
        Example 1.21 Expand <m>(1+2y)^5</m> using the binomial theorem. Hence
        use your results to evaluate <m>(1.04)^5</m>, correct to 4 decimal
        places.
      </p>

      <p><term>Solution</term></p>

      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            (1+2y)^5 &amp;= 1 + 5(2y) + \frac{5(4)}{2\times1}(2y)^2 +
            \frac{5(4)(3)}{3\times2\times1}(2y)^3
          </mrow>

          <mrow>
            &amp;\qquad + \frac{5(4)(3)(2)}{4\times3\times2\times1}(2y)^4 +
            \frac{5(4)(3)(2)(1)}{5\times4\times3\times2\times1}(2y)^5
          </mrow>
        </md>

        <md>= 1 + 5(2y) + 10(4y^2) + 10(8y^3) + 5(16y^4) + 1(32y^5)</md>

        <md>(1+2y)^5 = 1 + 10y + 40y^2 + 80y^3 + 80y^4 + 32y^5</md>
      </p>

      <p>
        <m>(1.04)^5</m> can be written as <m>(1+0.04)^5</m>. Comparing
        <m>(1.04)^5</m> to <m>(1+2y)^5</m>:

        <md>2y = 0.04 \implies y = 0.02</md>

        Substituting <m>y = 0.02</m> into the expansion:

        <md>
          1 + 10(0.02) + 40(0.02)^2 + 80(0.02)^3 + 80(0.02)^4 + 32(0.02)^5
        </md>

        <md>
          = 1 + 0.2 + 0.016 + 0.00064 + 0.0000128 + 0.0000001024 =
          1.2166529024
        </md>

        <md>\approx 1.2167 \text{ (to 4 d.p.)}</md>

        Thus <m>(1.04)^5 \approx 1.2167</m>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <example>
      <p>
        Example 1.22 Find the first five terms of the expression
        <m>\sqrt{1+3x}</m>. Hence find an estimate of the value of
        <m>\sqrt{1.12}</m> to 5 decimal places.
      </p>

      <p><term>Solution</term></p>

      <p>
        <md>\sqrt{1+3x} = (1+3x)^{1/2}</md>

        <md>
          <mrow>
            (1+3x)^{1/2} &amp;= 1 + \frac{1}{2}(3x) +
            \frac{\tfrac{1}{2}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}-1\right)}{2!}(3x)^2 +
            \frac{\tfrac{1}{2}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}-1\right)\left(\tfrac{1}{2}-2\right)}{3!}(3x)^3
          </mrow>

          <mrow>
            &amp;\quad +
            \frac{\tfrac{1}{2}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}-1\right)\left(\tfrac{1}{2}-2\right)\left(\tfrac{1}{2}-3\right)}{4!}(3x)^4
          </mrow>

          <mrow>
            &amp;= 1 + \frac{3x}{2} + \frac{-\tfrac{1}{4}}{2}(3x)^2 +
            \frac{\tfrac{3}{8}}{6}(3x)^3 + \frac{-\tfrac{15}{16}}{24}(3x)^4
          </mrow>

          <mrow>
            &amp;= 1 + \frac{3x}{2} - \frac{1}{8}(9x^2) + \frac{1}{48}(27x^3)
            - \frac{5}{128}(81x^4)
          </mrow>
        </md>

        <md>
          \sqrt{1+3x} = 1 + \frac{3x}{2} - \frac{9x^2}{8} + \frac{27x^3}{16} -
          \frac{405x^4}{128}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        <m>\sqrt{1.12}</m> can be written as <m>(1+0.12)^{1/2}</m>. Comparing
        <m>(1+0.12)^{1/2}</m> to <m>(1+3x)^{1/2}</m>:

        <md>3x = 0.12 \implies x = 0.04</md>

        Substituting <m>x = 0.04</m> into the expansion:

        <md>
          1 + \frac{3(0.04)}{2} - \frac{9(0.04)^2}{8} + \frac{27(0.04)^3}{16}
          - \frac{405(0.04)^4}{128}
        </md>

        <md>
          = 1 + \frac{0.12}{2} - \frac{0.0144}{8} + \frac{0.001728}{16} -
          \frac{0.00020736}{128}
        </md>

        <md>= 1 + 0.06 - 0.0018 + 0.000108 - 0.0000081 = 1.0582999</md>

        <md>\approx 1.05830 \text{ (to 5 d.p.)}</md>

        Thus <m>\sqrt{1.12} \approx 1.05830</m>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <example>
      <p>
        Example 1.23 Using the binomial theorem, find an estimate for the
        value of <m>\sqrt{101}</m>.
      </p>

      <p><term>Solution</term></p>

      <p>
        <md>
          \sqrt{101} = \sqrt{100+1} = (100+1)^{1/2} =
          100^{1/2}\left(1+\frac{1}{100}\right)^{1/2} =
          10\left(1+0.01\right)^{1/2}
        </md>

        <md>
          <mrow>
            10(1+0.01)^{1/2} &amp;= 10\left[1 + \frac{1}{2}(0.01) +
            \frac{\tfrac{1}{2}\left(-\tfrac{1}{2}\right)}{2!}(0.01)^2 +
            \frac{\tfrac{1}{2}\left(-\tfrac{1}{2}\right)\left(-\tfrac{3}{2}\right)}{3!}(0.01)^3
            + \cdots\right]
          </mrow>

          <mrow>
            &amp;= 10\left[1 + \frac{1}{2}(0.01) - \frac{1}{8}(0.0001) +
            \frac{1}{16}(0.000001)\right]
          </mrow>

          <mrow>
            &amp;= 10\left[1 + 0.005 - 0.0000125 + 0.0000000625\right]
          </mrow>

          <mrow>&amp;= 10\left[1.004987563\right] = 10.04987563</mrow>
        </md>

        Thus <m>\sqrt{101} \approx 10.0499</m>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <example>
      <p>Example 1.24 Use the binomial theorem to find approximations for:</p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><m>(3.97)^{1/2}</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>(0.994)^{1/4}</m></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>\sqrt{25.05}</m></p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p><term>Solution</term></p>

      <p>
        <term>a.</term>

        <md>
          (3.97)^{1/2} = (4-0.03)^{1/2} = 4^{1/2}(1-0.0075)^{1/2} =
          2(1-0.0075)^{1/2}
        </md>

        <md>
          <mrow>
            2(1-0.0075)^{1/2} &amp;= 2\left[1 + \frac{1}{2}(-0.0075) +
            \frac{\tfrac{1}{2}\left(-\tfrac{1}{2}\right)}{2!}(-0.0075)^2 +
            \cdots\right]
          </mrow>

          <mrow>
            &amp;= 2\left[1 - 0.00375 - \frac{1}{8}(0.0075)^2 + \cdots\right]
          </mrow>

          <mrow>&amp;= 2\left[1 - 0.00375 - 0.00003125 + \cdots\right]</mrow>

          <mrow>&amp;= 2\left[0.99621875\right] = 1.9924375</mrow>
        </md>

        <md>\therefore (3.97)^{1/2} \approx 1.992 \text{ (to 4 s.f.)}</md>
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>b.</term>

        <md>(0.994)^{1/4} = (1-0.006)^{1/4}</md>

        <md>
          <mrow>
            (1-0.006)^{1/4} &amp;= 1 + \frac{1}{4}(-0.006) +
            \frac{\tfrac{1}{4}\left(-\tfrac{3}{4}\right)}{2!}(-0.006)^2 +
            \cdots
          </mrow>

          <mrow>&amp;= 1 - 0.0015 - \frac{1}{32}(0.000036) + \cdots</mrow>

          <mrow>&amp;= 1 - 0.0015 - 0.000001125 + \cdots</mrow>

          <mrow>&amp;= 0.998498875</mrow>
        </md>

        <md>\therefore (0.994)^{1/4} \approx 0.9985 \text{ (to 4 s.f.)}</md>
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>c.</term>

        <md>
          \sqrt{25.05} = (25+0.05)^{1/2} = 25^{1/2}(1+0.002)^{1/2} =
          5(1+0.002)^{1/2}
        </md>

        <md>
          <mrow>
            5(1+0.002)^{1/2} &amp;= 5\left[1 + \frac{1}{2}(0.002) +
            \frac{\tfrac{1}{2}\left(-\tfrac{1}{2}\right)}{2!}(0.002)^2 +
            \cdots\right]
          </mrow>

          <mrow>
            &amp;= 5\left[1 + 0.001 - \frac{1}{8}(0.000004) + \cdots\right]
          </mrow>

          <mrow>
            &amp;= 5\left[1 + 0.001 - 0.0000005\right] =
            5\left[1.0009995\right]
          </mrow>

          <mrow>&amp;= 5.0049975</mrow>
        </md>

        <md>\therefore \sqrt{25.05} \approx 5.005 \text{ (to 4 s.f.)}</md>
      </p>
    </example>
  </introduction>

